# Repo-root .dockerignore for detector/Dockerfile (Task C3).
#
# IMPORTANT -- what this file does and does NOT protect, verified by
# searching the OpenShift docs source (openshift/openshift-docs,
# enterprise-4.20 branch, 2026-08-08: zero hits for "dockerignore" anywhere
# in that repo):
#
#   * Standard `docker build` / `podman build -f detector/Dockerfile .` run
#     from the repo root DOES respect this file (excludes these paths from
#     the build context entirely) -- useful for local testing of the image.
#   * Buildah, which executes the actual image build inside an OpenShift
#     BuildConfig's build pod, also honors a `.dockerignore` at the context
#     root for its own COPY/ADD instructions.
#   * What is NOT confirmed anywhere in the fetched OpenShift 4.20 docs is
#     whether `oc start-build --from-dir=DIR` filters DIR by this file
#     *before* archiving and uploading it to the build -- the docs describe
#     `--from-dir` purely as "contents are archived and sent as a binary
#     stream" with no mention of dockerignore-aware filtering at that step.
#
# Because `cluster/` holds LIVE, gitignored OpenShift credentials (kubeconfig,
# kubeadmin password, installer TLS material -- see repo-root .gitignore and
# AGENTS.md #3, "never commit, print, echo, or paste their contents"), this
# repo's runbook (deploy/phase3/README.md, "Build context safety") does NOT
# rely on this file alone to keep `cluster/` out of a binary build upload --
# it stages a directory containing only `dist/` + `detector/` and runs
# `oc start-build --from-dir=` against THAT, so the live credentials never
# leave the workstation regardless of how `oc` handles this file. This
# .dockerignore is kept anyway as defense in depth for local
# docker/podman builds and for the in-pod Buildah COPY step.
cluster/
research/
benchmarks/data/
benchmarks/results/
.git/
gpu/
*.pem
*.key
aws
